>>
endobj
83 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (In cities and nearby towns everyday life produces a lot of solid waste.. Municipal cleanup teams are usually slow, short on staff and respond only after problems arise instead of preventing them. Dumping sites next to markets, bus stops, drains and parks often stay unchecked for weeks. The reason is that there is no system that links the people who see the problem the workers who could clean it and the money required to do it. City authorities also do not have location-specific information about where waste really piles up so they cannot plan cleanup actions, in the best order.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
84 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (CleanLink fills this gap by launching a community-led cleanup and micro-job platform. Citizens can point out waste spots by uploading pictures. The community and moderators check these reports. The platform then allows businesses, NGOs and private people to fund the estimated cost of cleaning. When money is secured students or gig workers can sign up for the task. Before pictures prove the work is finished CleanLink releases the payment.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
85 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (This issue matters for three reasons. First uncontrolled waste poses a public health risk. It attracts disease carriers. Pollutes water. Second it hurts the environment. Ruins public areas. Third it hides)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
86 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (a chance. Informal and gig workers could earn money from verified cleanup jobs. No easy platform links them to paid, local work.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
87 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (By merging citizen reports, community funding and verified micro-jobs CleanLink shifts waste man- agement from a passive municipal duty to an active, data-powered community-driven effort. This gives both people and local governments a quicker way to keep neighborhoods clean.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
