/E (State of the Art / Current solution \(1 pt\))
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
93 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 46 0 R
/E (Today waste management in Indian towns and cities relies on manual municipal processes. San- itation staff follow fixed routes and schedules and residents report problems if all through phone helplines, generic complaint apps or word of mouth to local officials. Some cities use apps such as Swachhata-MoHUA or local municipal grievance portals. These apps let citizens file complaints with photos. Waste management typically ends at the report stage. There is no visibility into funding no connection to available workers and no verification loop to confirm that the waste problem was actually resolved.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
94 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 46 0 R
/E (Crowdfunding platforms such as Ketto and Milaap exist for causes but they are not built for local task-specific problems such as a single overflowing dumping ground. Similarly gig-work platforms such as Urban Company and local labor apps connect workers to paid jobs. They have no waste-reporting or community-verification component built in.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
95 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 46 0 R
/E (As a result the current waste management solution is fragmented across three systems: complaint reporting, fundraising and gig work. No platform integrates all three, around a verified outcome. That outcome is a location that gets reported, funded cleaned and confirmed clean.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
